<section class="rs-section" aria-labelledby="rs-title">
  <div class="container rs-inner">
  <header class="rs-rail" data-reveal>
    <p class="rs-num">02</p>
    <h2 id="rs-title" class="rs-title">Research approach</h2>
  </header>

  <div class="rs-body" data-reveal style="--i:1">
    <div class="rs-group">
      <p class="rs-p">We needed both sides of the problem: how students actually use AI, and what their professors notice once those students have to work without it. So we ran three strands of research, each answering a different question.</p>

      <dl class="rs-methods">
        <div class="rs-method">
          <dt>Literature review</dt>
          <dd>What's already known. We looked at Khanmigo and Duolingo's AI tutors to understand the opportunities and risks of AI-supported learning.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rs-method">
          <dt>Student survey</dt>
          <dd>How students use AI for learning today, and where it lets them down.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rs-method">
          <dt>Professor interviews</dt>
          <dd>What educators see in the work students hand in, and in how they think in class.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="rs-p">Before writing our survey and interview questions, we looked at what researchers and educators already knew. The literature showed that AI can help with feedback and personalized practice, but it also raised two risks for learning: <strong>automation bias</strong>, when people trust an AI answer because it sounds authoritative, and <strong>confirmation bias</strong>, when they accept information that reinforces what they already believe.<sup class="rs-cite"><a href="#rs-refs">1,2</a></sup> We used those risks to shape the questions we asked students and professors.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rs-group">
      <h3 class="rs-h3">What students told us</h3>

      <div class="rs-stats">
        <div class="rs-stat">
          <span class="rs-stat-num rs-stat-num--accent">82%</span>
          <span class="rs-stat-label">use AI mainly for speed and efficiency</span>
        </div>
        <div class="rs-stat">
          <span class="rs-stat-num">3.6%</span>
          <span class="rs-stat-label">use it on purpose to understand more deeply</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="rs-p">Our research showed us how that tension played out for students. They were turning to AI mainly to move faster, not to understand more deeply, which made the risks from the literature feel concrete. <strong>Some told us they feel guilty</strong> handing in work they don't fully understand. Others pointed to inaccurate answers and not knowing how to write a good prompt. Students wanted help in the moment, but their frustrations suggested they needed more than a faster answer.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rs-group">
      <h3 class="rs-h3">What professors saw</h3>
      <p class="rs-p">Professors described the same habit from the other side. To them, the issue isn't that students use AI. It's that the tool skips the part of learning where the student has to think.</p>

      <figure class="rs-quote">
        <blockquote>Students don't think AI is a collaboration tool, they think of it as a do-my-homework tool.</blockquote>
        <figcaption>Professor interview</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="rs-quote">
        <blockquote>I ask questions, then follow up with more questions, that's how students start thinking. AI skips that process.</blockquote>
        <figcaption>Professor interview</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="rs-core">
      <p class="rs-core-label">The core problem</p>
      <p class="rs-core-text">AI tools give an answer right away and skip the back-and-forth questioning that deep learning depends on.</p>
    </div>

    <ol id="rs-refs" class="rs-refs">
      <li><a href="https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark:/48223/pf0000386693">UNESCO, Guidance for generative AI in education and research</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lindif.2023.102274">Kasneci et al., ChatGPT for good? On opportunities and challenges of large language models for education</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

